To solve this problem, we need to consider all possible ways to partition the number N such that the number of petals in the bouquet equals the number of pieces of chocolate in the basket. For each type of flower, we can calculate how many valid combinations of flower types we can have to make up the total number of petals. Similarly, for each type of box, we do the same for the total number of pieces of chocolate.

The number of valid combinations for a given flower type is given by factoring N into parts where each part is a divisor of piblah where blah is the type of flower. The number of ways to partition N by this divisor is given by the coefficient of xN in the expansion of (1 + x)^(divcount(piblah)) where divcount is the number of divisors.

We then sum up the contributions from all flower types and all box types to get the total number of combinations, taking modulo 10^9 + 7.

